 **Meta (Facebook Pages + Instagram).** Create a Meta developer app with *Facebook Login for Business* (or Facebook Login), add the Messenger and Instagram products, and set:
@@ -170,8 +177,30 @@ One AgenticSales app per platform, registered once by us; then each owner connec
 - Permissions we request: `pages_show_list, pages_manage_metadata, pages_messaging, pages_manage_posts, pages_read_engagement, instagram_basic, instagram_content_publish, instagram_manage_messages`. Until Meta approves the app (App Review + Business Verification), only accounts with a role on the app can connect; real customers need the review.
 - Instagram posting and messaging require an Instagram **professional** account linked to a Facebook Page.
 
+**Instagram Login (no Facebook Page needed).** This is the "Instagram API with Instagram Login" product of the same Meta app, and it has its **own** app id and secret (Instagram product -> API setup with Instagram login). Owners only need an Instagram professional account. Set:
+- Redirect URI (Instagram Login -> business login settings): `{PUBLIC_BASE_URL}/connect/callback/instagram`
+- Webhook: the same callback URL as Facebook, `{PUBLIC_BASE_URL}/webhooks/meta`, and the same verify token (shown in the operator console). Subscribe the Instagram object to `messages`. Events are signed with the Instagram app secret; the shared webhook accepts either secret.
+- Permissions: `instagram_business_basic`, `instagram_business_manage_messages`, `instagram_business_content_publish`. In development mode, add each tester's Instagram account under the app's roles (Instagram Testers) and have them accept the invite.
+- Tokens last 60 days; we renew them when under 10 days remain (on use). An account connected this way sends and posts through `graph.instagram.com`; accounts connected through Facebook keep using `graph.facebook.com`.
+
+**Threads.** The "Access the Threads API" use case of the Meta app has its **own** app id and secret. Set the redirect URI (Threads settings -> redirect callback URLs) to `{PUBLIC_BASE_URL}/connect/callback/threads`, request `threads_basic` and `threads_content_publish`, and add each tester under the app's roles (Threads Testers) and have them accept the invite on Threads. Tokens last 60 days and are renewed on use. Threads has no direct-messages API, so connecting gives posting only (text and single-image posts of up to 500 characters); replies are a possible later addition (`threads_manage_replies`).
+
 **X.** In the X developer portal create a project and app with OAuth 2.0 *confidential client* (Web App), user authentication on, scopes `tweet.read tweet.write users.read offline.access`, and callback URL `{PUBLIC_BASE_URL}/connect/callback/x`. X's API is pay-per-use: check current pricing before onboarding a business. Receiving X DMs needs X's Account Activity webhooks and is not part of this flow; connecting gives posting.
 
+**TikTok.** In the TikTok developer portal (developers.tiktok.com) create an app, add the **Login Kit** product (and **Content Posting API** for `video.upload`), and set:
+- Redirect URI (Login Kit, Web): `{PUBLIC_BASE_URL}/connect/callback/tiktok`. It must be HTTPS and match exactly, with no trailing slash or query string.
+- Scopes: login needs only `user.info.basic`. To post videos from the console, add the **Content Posting API** product with **Direct Post** enabled, then set `TIKTOK_SCOPES=user.info.basic,video.publish` and have each owner reconnect once (an old token does not carry a new scope). Every scope in `TIKTOK_SCOPES` must exist on the app or TikTok rejects the whole login. Until TikTok audits the app, direct posts are visible only to the account owner (the publisher uses the most open privacy level TikTok offers the account, so this changes by itself after the audit).
+- **Sandbox:** create a Sandbox in the app, copy its client key and secret (they differ from production), and under *Target users* add the TikTok account that will sign in. Only listed target users can authorise a sandbox app.
+- TikTok access tokens last 24 hours; we refresh them just before use (refresh tokens last about a year, after which the owner sees Reconnect). TikTok has no public DM API, so connecting gives sign-in and video posting only; messaging stays unavailable. The owner attaches a video to a TikTok post in the Content page, and "Publish now" uploads it to TikTok and waits for TikTok to confirm.
+
+**Telegram (no approval, no verification).** A business creates a bot with Telegram's @BotFather (`/newbot`), pastes the token under Settings, Channels, Telegram, and is connected: we check the token, register `{PUBLIC_BASE_URL}/webhooks/<business-id>/telegram` with a secret, and store the token encrypted. Customers message the bot and the agent answers text, voice notes (converted with ffmpeg) and photos exactly as on the other channels. Only private chats are answered. Telegram needs nothing configured on the API host except `PUBLIC_BASE_URL` (HTTPS, a certificate Telegram trusts: Caddy's is). Tell the owner to revoke a token that was ever pasted into a chat or ticket (BotFather `/revoke`) and paste the new one.
+
+**Public web chat.** A business that has published its landing page (Settings, landing page) also has a public chat at `{PUBLIC_BASE_URL}/chat/<business-id>`, linked from that page by a "Chat with us here" button (rebuild the page to add it). Anyone can open it with no account: they can type, attach a photo or record a voice note, and the agent answers exactly as on the other channels. It says it is a virtual assistant and that a team member can step in. Visitors are limited to 12 messages a minute and a business to 100 a minute, and every reply counts against the business's plan like any AI reply. It needs no platform approval, so it works while the Meta channels wait for verification. Embed it with `<iframe src=".../chat/<id>" allow="microphone">`.
+
+**Legal pages (needed before a Meta, Instagram, Threads or TikTok app can be published).** The API serves a privacy policy, terms of service and data-deletion page with no key at `{PUBLIC_BASE_URL}/legal/privacy`, `/legal/terms` and `/legal/data-deletion`. Paste those into the app settings of each platform (Privacy Policy URL, Terms of Service URL, User data deletion). Set `LEGAL_ENTITY` (the name shown in the text; default "AgenticSales") and `LEGAL_CONTACT_EMAIL` (the address people write to for questions and deletion requests). The text describes what the system does and is a starting point, not legal advice: have it reviewed before real customers are served. Note that Meta delivers only dashboard test webhooks while an app is unpublished, so real Instagram DMs need the app published.
+
+**Muse Spark and voice notes.** Set `META_MODEL_API_KEY` (a key from dev.meta.ai) and `MODEL_PROVIDER=meta` to answer with Muse Spark and to transcribe voice notes with Muse Voice Transcribe. Muse Spark reasons silently before answering, which costs time and tokens, so `CONVERSATION_EFFORT` and `REASONING_EFFORT` (default `low`) and `EXTRACTOR_EFFORT` (default `minimal`) accept `minimal`, `low`, `medium` or `high`; the provider adds matching token headroom so an answer is never squeezed out. Customer photos are read with the same model (`VISION_MODEL`, `VISION_EFFORT`, default `minimal`, which was as accurate as `low` in testing and about 2 seconds faster). The transcriber accepts only mono 16-bit WAV at 16 or 24 kHz, so incoming voice notes (OGG/Opus on WhatsApp) are converted with ffmpeg, which the Docker image installs (`FFMPEG_PATH` points at a specific binary if it is not on PATH).
+
 **WhatsApp** is not part of one-click connect (Meta's Embedded Signup is a separate flow, not built yet); an operator enters its credentials in the operator console.
 
 The operator console's home page shows, per provider, whether the app is configured and the exact redirect URI, webhook URL and verify token to register. Check with `GET /v1/connect/providers` (a business key) after setting the variables.
